WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:06.480
Take a look back through history and you'll&nbsp;
find no shortage of famous figures who blur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:14.100
the boundaries between fact and fiction. Think&nbsp;
Leonidas I of Sparta or King Midas - both were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.660
100% real, but it's safe to say many of the&nbsp;
stories surrounding them probably weren't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.380
Most of these titans of times gone by&nbsp;
only rose to true world fame after their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.180
tales were corrupted and endlessly&nbsp;
embelished, long after their deaths.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.920
But every once in a while, someone&nbsp;
emerges from the haze of history whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.660
dazzling deeds are so inexplicable they&nbsp;
become legends within their own lifetime.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.960
The subject of today's video is one such man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.880
and his story is quite simply one of&nbsp;
the most unbelievable I've ever shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.620
on this channel. It's hard to know where to&nbsp;
begin really, so how about some highlights.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.540
1. He was born a peasant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:00.240
but rose to control the whole of Russia
2. He had the power to heal the sick and the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.000
doctors of his time couldn't explain how he did it
3. He survived multiple assassination attempts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.020
including being poisoned, stabbed, and shot
4. He had the power of prophecy, and made several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.240
bold predictions that subsequently came true
5. He slept with thousands of women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.980
despite never bathing
6. He brought down a 300-year old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:28.440
Royal dynasty almost singlehandedly
7. And, most importantly of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.420
his pickled penis is preserved&nbsp;
in a Russian museum to this day

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.940
This is the larger than life&nbsp;
story of Grigori Rasputin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.820
or as I like to call him, the magical sex&nbsp;
wizard who brought down the Russian monarchy.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.820
Rasputin's story is one of the most outlandish&nbsp;
ever told, but his beginnings were about as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.580
humble as it gets. He was born a peasant in 1869&nbsp;
in the tiny Siberian village of Pokrovskoye.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.800
He had seven siblings, but only Rasputin
made it to adulthood. Very little is known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.920
about those early years beyond the&nbsp;
fact that, like most of his peers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.240
he received little in the way of education.&nbsp;
It's likely he could neither read nor write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.080
until adulthood, and he was regularly&nbsp;
in trouble with the local authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:37.320
thanks to a penchant for committing minor&nbsp;
crimes and a major appetite for hard drink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:43.680
and soft women. By 18 he was married and&nbsp;
would soon raise 3 children of his own.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.560
So far so utterly boring - at this point&nbsp;
in our story Rasputin's life wasn't all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.680
that dissimilar to thousands of other Russian&nbsp;
men of the era. But in 1897 at the age of 28,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.960
he decided to take a pilgrimage to the St.&nbsp;
Nicholas Monastery at Verkhoturye. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.840
a journey that would dramatically alter both&nbsp;
his life and the course of Russian history.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.980
Rasputin wasn't an especially religious man&nbsp;
prior to the pilgrimage, and his reasons for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.780
undertaking a 500 mile round trip in the name&nbsp;
of a God he wasn't all that well acquainted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:24.540
with are unknown. Some sources claim it&nbsp;
was an excuse to make a swift exit from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:30.840
Pokrovskoye after being caught stealing a&nbsp;
horse - but I say that the heavy drinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:37.860
wantonly womanising Rasputin took the pilgrimage&nbsp;
because lads weekends hadn't been invented yet.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.460
Whatever the truth, when Rasputin returned&nbsp;
some months later he was a man transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.920
by religious fervour. Well, I say&nbsp;
'transformed' - he still drank like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.080
a sailor and chased women night and day, he&nbsp;
just did a lot more praying in his downtime.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.940
Over the following few years, he travelled&nbsp;
Russia extensively, styling himself as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.800
wandering holy man. He also developed his&nbsp;
own unique brand of religion built on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.080
foundations of an outlawed Christian sect&nbsp;
known as the Khlysts, whose murky practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.840
were rumoured to included mass orgies and&nbsp;
self flagellation (not at the same time).

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.960
For his part, Rasputin preached that sin could&nbsp;
only be overcome if it was first understood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.240
which basically gave him free rein to do all&nbsp;
the fun stuff life has to offer in the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:34.260
of theological research. He also taught that it&nbsp;
was possible to get closer to God by achieving a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:40.500
state of sexual exhaustion, preferably by having&nbsp;
as much sex as possible with Rasputin himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.440
Bearing in mind that he was one of the&nbsp;
scariest-looking individuals to ever live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.440
and was famous for never washing and therefore&nbsp;
smelled like a badger's arsehole, you might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:58.680
surprised to learn that, somehow, this strange&nbsp;
strategy for seducing women actually worked..

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.020
Rasputin soon gathered a large number&nbsp;
of followers, and there were growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:10.680
whispers that, during his time in the Siberian&nbsp;
wilderness, he’d developed certain… abilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:17.160
Some said he could read minds, others that he&nbsp;
could heal the sick with the touch of a hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.860
and all agreed that there was&nbsp;
something otherworldly about the man.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.040
By the early 1900s, he'd caused enough of a stir&nbsp;
to attract the attention of several important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:36.360
people in the Russian Orthodox Church. Eventually,&nbsp;
those lofty connections led him to St. Petersburg.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:42.780
Rasputin's arrival in the then-Russian&nbsp;
capital was perfectly timed. The city's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.220
aristocracy were positively obsessed&nbsp;
with all things occult and supernatural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:54.240
so when the badger-scented Siberian turned&nbsp;
up amid rumours of magical powers in 1905&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.820
armed with a soul-piercing stare,&nbsp;
everyone wanted a piece of him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.900
He received invitations from many of the most&nbsp;
influential and affluent families in the city.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:13.260
Rung by rung, he hauled himself up St.&nbsp;
Petersburg's teetering social ladder until,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:21.900
in November 1905, he reached the final boss&nbsp;
- he was invited to meet Tsar Nicholas II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:28.320
Emperor of all of Russia and patriarch&nbsp;
of the centuries old Romanov family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.580
and his wife Alexandra Feodorovna, the Tsarina.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.180
The encounter was planned for just a few minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.920
but the self-proclaimed mystic worked his&nbsp;
unique brand of black magic on the monarchs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.100
and it was well over an hour&nbsp;
before he emerged from the palace.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:50.040
It's often said that people believe what they want&nbsp;
to believe, and that no doubt helped Rasputin win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:55.260
over the Russian royals. You see, the Tsar and&nbsp;
Tsarina were absolutely desperate for the rumours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.260 --> 00:08:02.400
about Rasputin to be true. Because if he really&nbsp;
could heal the sick, that meant he had the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:08.520
to save their son, Alexei, who also happened&nbsp;
to be the heir to the entire Russian empire.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.400
Alexei suffered from haemophilia, a rare&nbsp;
genetic disorder that prevents the blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.020
from clotting properly. That might sound like&nbsp;
a fairly minor ailment, but sufferers live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:24.540
their entire lives on a knife-edge, where even a&nbsp;
small injury can lead to devastating blood loss.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:30.540
Haemophilia is a serious illness even&nbsp;
today, but in 20th century Russia it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.800
was essentially a death sentence - the&nbsp;
average life expectancy was just 13.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:42.600
Alexei was the youngest of 5 children, but since&nbsp;
his elder siblings were all inconveniently female,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.880
the line of succession relied solely&nbsp;
on his survival. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:54.060
if Rasputin could keep Alexei alive, he wasn't&nbsp;
just saving the life of a treasured son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:59.100
he was safeguarding the future&nbsp;
of the entire Romanov dynasty.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.940
And it wouldn't be long before the holy man was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.000
given a chance to put his&nbsp;
magic where his mouth was.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:13.380
As an interesting aside, at the time haemophilia&nbsp;
was so common among European royalty that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:18.300
often referred to as the 'royal disease'.&nbsp;
Historians have subsequently traced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:23.700
rogue genes back to a single 'patient zero'&nbsp;
- none other than the so-called ‘grandmother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.620
of Europe’ Queen Victoria. She was&nbsp;
a carrier, and her offspring spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.180
haemophilia around Europe's aristocracy,&nbsp;
including the royal families of Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.220
Germany, and Russia (Queen Victoria was&nbsp;
Alexei Romanov's great grandmother).

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:45.060
Anyway, Rasputin was duly summoned to use his&nbsp;
magic powers on the boy, and here's where things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:51.060
start to get a little weird. It's easy to dismiss&nbsp;
Rasputin as a fraud - a charismatic chancer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:56.280
somehow bluffed his way to the top. But whatever&nbsp;
hocus pocus he employed on the Russian heir...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.340
it actually worked. And not just once&nbsp;
- Alexei's chief physician would later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:06.660
recall that whenever the boy was ill,&nbsp;
Rasputin would turn up at his bedside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:10.500
spit, and Alexei would recover in no time.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.440
In one of the more famous incidents, the prince&nbsp;
suffered internal bleeding during a bumpy carriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:21.720
ride back from a hunting trip. Before long he'd&nbsp;
fallen so gravely ill that a priest was summoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:27.720
to perform his last rites and a hasty telegram was&nbsp;
penned announcing the boy's death to the world.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:34.020
Rasputin was back home in Siberia at the&nbsp;
time, and the desperate Tsarina sent him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:39.720
a telegram of her own asking him to pray for&nbsp;
her son. Despite being hundreds of miles away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:44.460
Rasputin replied explaining that&nbsp;
God had personally assured him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.620
the prince would live so long as no&nbsp;
doctors interfered in his recovery.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.900
I don't know about you, but if&nbsp;
I was a fraudulent faith healer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.900 --> 00:11:00.300
getting rid everyone with a modicum of medical&nbsp;
knowledge would be the last thing I'd do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:10.020
But the Tsarina obliged, and, against all common&nbsp;
sense, Alexei began to recover almost immediately.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:17.940
Even today, more than 100 years later, nobody&nbsp;
really knows exactly what Rasputin was doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:23.760
to improve Alexei’s health. It could be he was&nbsp;
leveraging knowledge of internal bleeding cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:30.900
in horses from his time in Siberia, while others&nbsp;
believe his cures relied on some form of hypnosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:36.060
Still others put his miraculous healing&nbsp;
powers down to nothing more than simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:42.660
psychology - by dismissing the Prince's army of&nbsp;
doctors and calming his mother's frayed nerves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:49.440
Rasputin may simply have created a calmer,&nbsp;
quieter atmosphere more conducive to recovery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:54.180
Add a powerful placebo effect&nbsp;
into the mix and who knows...

00:11:54.180 --> 00:12:01.500
If that's all a bit vague for you, don’t worry,&nbsp;
I have a slightly more concrete theory. Aspirin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:05.520
was a ubiquitous painkiller in Russia at the&nbsp;
time, and would almost certainly have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:11.400
prescribed for Alexei. But it turns out that&nbsp;
aspirin thins the blood and is basically oral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.980
kryptonite for haemophiliacs. That little&nbsp;
medical factoid wasn't known to science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:23.520
until a good 50 years after Rasputin's&nbsp;
death, but it may be that by regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:29.700
recommending Alexei's doctor's be ignored in&nbsp;
favour of the healing power of God's grace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:37.440
Rasputin was unwittingly protecting the prince&nbsp;
from the harmful effects of ingesting aspirin.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.300
Of course, there's always the possibility&nbsp;
that Rasputin really had gained magical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:46.320
healing powers by shagging his way&nbsp;
around Russia and refusing to bathe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.380
but I'm going to go ahead and&nbsp;
put that one down as a long shot.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:55.020
Ultimately it doesn't really matter how he was&nbsp;
doing it. By ensuring blood flowed through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.020
prince’s veins - and more importantly&nbsp;
by keeping it there whenever he got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:04.020
so much as paper cut - Rasputin gained the&nbsp;
unshakeable loyalty of the Tsar and Tsarina.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:11.640
Still, Rasputin wasn't one to take chances, and so&nbsp;
to truly seal his position in the royal household,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:19.860
this big-dicked mystic made a prophecy, claiming&nbsp;
that his destiny was linked with theirs and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:24.960
if anything untoward ever happened to&nbsp;
him, the house of Romanov would fall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:29.160
Again, it's easy to scoff&nbsp;
at such an obvious ploy...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:35.460
except the prophecy would indeed later come&nbsp;
true - but let's not get ahead of ourselves.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:42.480
Tsar Nicholas II named Rasputin as his official&nbsp;
lamplighter, a role which saw him look after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:48.420
royal household’s religious icons and gave him&nbsp;
unparalleled access to Russia's rulers. Rasputin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:52.980
made the most of his position, and before&nbsp;
long he wasn't just healing Prince Alexei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:57.720
he was advising on matters of state&nbsp;
and influencing foreign policy.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:02.520
But his elevated status had done nothing&nbsp;
to smooth Rasputin's rough manners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:09.120
nor diminish any of his various... appetites.&nbsp;
Rumours abounded that the mystic would accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:14.760
sexual favours and other bribes in return for&nbsp;
putting in a good word with the Tsar and his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:20.880
and when he couldn't get what he wanted by abusing&nbsp;
his newfoundpower, he simply took it by force.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:26.280
Rasputin was accused of rape on multiple&nbsp;
occasions, including by the governess who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:31.680
looked after Alexei's older sisters. But&nbsp;
20th century Russia's version of the MeToo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:36.540
movement never really got off the ground.&nbsp;
Despite the weight of claims against him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:41.820
including some from within the royal&nbsp;
family itself, Rasputin was untouchable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:47.520
The governess was fired, and his&nbsp;
other accusers simply ignored.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:54.540
Even so, a growing faction of religious and&nbsp;
political leaders were becoming increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.940
concerned about Rasputin's influence in the&nbsp;
palace. Several senior members of the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:05.280
Orthodox Church openly denounced him as a&nbsp;
heretic, and Prime Minister Peter Stolypin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:11.760
tried to get him exiled. The Russian secret&nbsp;
police - a precursor of the KGB - opened an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:16.200
investigation into Rasputin's conduct&nbsp;
which found, among many other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:20.820
that he'd exposed himself in one of&nbsp;
St. Petersburg's best restaurants.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:28.080
In 1914 while visiting his wife (who for&nbsp;
some reason hadn't divorced him yet),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:33.720
Rasputin was stabbed in the stomach by a noseless&nbsp;
peasant woman who believed he was the antichrist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:40.560
It was the kind of wound that would typically be&nbsp;
fatal, and after somehow escaping his attacker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:48.300
on foot, Rasputin was left in a coma that&nbsp;
nobody expected him to wake from. But he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:54.360
did. And before long he was back in the capital,&nbsp;
where his power was about to reach its pinnacle.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:16:01.500
Just a few weeks before Rasputin's near death&nbsp;
experience, a little known Austro-Hungarian royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:08.160
was visited by an assassin of his own. The royal&nbsp;
in question was known to his buddies as Archduke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:16.380
Franz Ferdinand, and, unlike Rasputin, he didn’t&nbsp;
survive the hit. As you're no doubt aware, Franz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:20.460
Ferdinand's death set off a chain of events that&nbsp;
culminated in the start of the First World War.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:28.320
In July 1914, Germany declared war on Russia, and&nbsp;
Tsar Nicholas II left St. Petersburg to personally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:33.840
lead his troops on the battlefields of Europe.&nbsp;
Leaving only his wife behind to run the country.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.520
Alexandra had always been more swayed by Rasputin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:44.580
and without her husband around she gave her&nbsp;
mind to the mystic entirely. Within the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:51.360
of 18 months she'd appointed and dismissed no&nbsp;
fewer than four prime ministers, five ministers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:58.080
of the interior, and three ministers of war,&nbsp;
with Rasputin guiding her hand at every turn.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:06.000
It was soon an open secret on the streets of St.&nbsp;
Petersburg that, along with accepting Rasputin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:12.660
advice, the Tsarina had also invited him into her&nbsp;
bed. Admittedly there isn't any hard evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:18.720
the affair ever happened, but considering how&nbsp;
highly the Tsarina regarded Rasputin - not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:24.660
mention that his religious ideology was to screw&nbsp;
every living thing in Russia - it’s difficult not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:31.320
to believe the rumours. There's also the fact&nbsp;
that renowned scholar Boney M famously sang 'Ra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:38.100
Ra Rasputin, lover of the Russian Queen.' And&nbsp;
if that isn't proof then I don't know what is.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:44.100
Regardless of whether or not they were actually&nbsp;
sleeping together, Russia’s greatest love machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:51.240
had clearly become the true power behind&nbsp;
the throne - the Wormtongue to Alexandra's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:57.540
Théoden. But every Wormtongue eventually&nbsp;
meets his Gandalf, and in December 1916,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:03.900
a group of noblemen decided it was time to&nbsp;
saddle up Greyfax and go drive the demon out.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:09.540
They were led by Prince Felix Yusupov&nbsp;
(husband of the Tsar's niece), Grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:16.800
Duke Dmitri Pavlovich (the Tsar's first cousin),&nbsp;
and prominent politician Vladimir Purishkevich.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:21.660
Of course, unlike Gandalf the Grey none of&nbsp;
these men actually had any magical powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:27.000
and so they were forced to chose a&nbsp;
more prosaic approach - to save Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.720
they were going to have to&nbsp;
murder Grigori Rasputin.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.040
But as we’ve already seen, Rasputin&nbsp;
was not an easy man to kill.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:35.820
He was invited to Yusopov's palace in&nbsp;
St. Petersburg, where he was taken to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:41.220
receiving room in the basement. There, he was&nbsp;
given tea and a platter of small cakes. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:46.080
all sounds very civil as far as murders go...&nbsp;
except the cakes had been laced with cyanide.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:52.380
Rasputin was oblivious to the danger, and soon&nbsp;
enough he was tucking into the poisonous pastries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:59.940
But when he was finished, rather than, you know,&nbsp;
drop dead, Rasputin merely asked if Yusopov had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:06.660
anything stronger than tea to wash down the cakes.&nbsp;
Yusopov fetched some Madeira wine - also laced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:13.200
with cyanide - but despite guzzling three glasses,&nbsp;
Rasputin didn't develop so much as heartburn.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:20.400
To this day we still have absolutely no idea how&nbsp;
Rasputin ate enough Cyanide to kill an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:27.000
village and lived - though in a very recent&nbsp;
study rats were fed both cyanide and garlic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.760
and survived. Turns out the sulfur content&nbsp;
of garlic can combat cyanide in the body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:38.580
and every day Rasputin ate a&nbsp;
frightening amount of garlic.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:45.480
Anyway, since the poison had failed to, well,&nbsp;
poison him, Yusopov pulled out his pistol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:51.060
and shot Rasputin squarely in the chest,&nbsp;
and that finally seemed to do the trick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:57.120
Rasputin’s body was stripped, and one of&nbsp;
the co conspirators drove to Rasputin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:01.380
house wearing the dead man's clothes to make it&nbsp;
look as though he'd returned home as planned.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:08.100
Next it was time to dispose of the corpse. That&nbsp;
was Yusopov's job, but when he went back down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:14.400
the cellar, something rather unexpected happened&nbsp;
- Rasputin stood up and punched him in the face.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:22.200
Yusopov fled the basement with a naked and very&nbsp;
much not-dead Rasputin in hot pursuit. A naked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:28.140
man running is never a pretty sight, and the&nbsp;
bloody, grizzled Rasputin was no exception. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:32.040
all the commotion brought Yusopov's friends&nbsp;
to the scene, and more shots were fired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:42.060
When the smoke had cleared, Rasputin was once more&nbsp;
on the ground, this time with a bullet through his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:48.180
forehead. It's safe to assume Yusopov and Co were&nbsp;
a little rattled at this point, so they spent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:52.860
next few minutes kicking the crap out of the&nbsp;
corpse, just to make sure it really *was* a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:59.760
corpse. Then they dumping the body in a nearby&nbsp;
frozen river, by way of a fishing hole. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:06.300
to some reports, despite having been poisoned&nbsp;
(twice), shot multiple times, and beaten to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:12.480
a bloody pulp, Rasputin was still moving when&nbsp;
he disappeared beneath the silent black water.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:18.840
He was found the following day, pressed up&nbsp;
against the underside of the ice as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:28.740
trying to break free, his still open eyes staring&nbsp;
blankly up towards the sky. The mad monk was dead.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:33.000
Meanwhile on the battlefields of Europe,&nbsp;
things were going badly for the Russians.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.960
Nicholas II was turning out to be an&nbsp;
absolutely useless military commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:42.420
and a series of serious tactical and logistical&nbsp;
blunders saw his troops lose battle after battle.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:47.790
The situation back home wasn't much better either.&nbsp;
The country had been crippled by the war effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:53.220
and there was widespread hunger and discontent.&nbsp;
Confidence in the monarchy was at an all time low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.220 --> 00:22:00.060
and despite trying to hang onto power, in&nbsp;
1917, the Tsar was forced to abdicate when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:05.460
angry demonstrators took over large swathes of&nbsp;
the capital backed by members of the Russian armed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:13.740
forces. The 300 year rule of the Romanovs was&nbsp;
at an end, and the Russian revolution had begun.

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:19.020
The Tsar and his family were held under house&nbsp;
arrest, where they would remain for about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:22.140
a year. During which time the Bolsheviks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:29.520
led by Vladimir Lenin, seized control of the&nbsp;
Russian government. On the 17th July 1918,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:35.460
Bolshevik soldiers broke into a basement where&nbsp;
the Romanovs were being held and started firing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:43.320
They didn't stop until the former Tsar and Tsarina&nbsp;
and every single one of their children were dead.

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:49.920
At the start of this video, I mentioned that&nbsp;
Rasputin was a man who blurred the lines between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:54.960
myth and reality, and there are certainly many&nbsp;
aspects of this tale that stretch credulity.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:23:00.300
Certain elements are widely accepted fact&nbsp;
- Rasputin really did rise from nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:05.040
to have a huge influence over the Russian&nbsp;
monarchy, and he certainly played a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:12.120
role in its eventual downfall. But others are&nbsp;
contested. Some historians believe details of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:16.920
Rasputin's debaucherous lifestyle were&nbsp;
exaggerated in order to discredit him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:21.540
and it seems a fairly safe bet that the events&nbsp;
of his murder have been… spiced up a little bit.&nbsp;

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.700
Oh, and for those of you still wondering when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:28.320
the hell I'm going to get to Rasputin’s&nbsp;
penis, don't worry - the wait is over.

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:35.400
The fate of Rasputin's pecker remains a&nbsp;
surprisingly hot topic even now. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:41.040
one story, it was removed not long after his death&nbsp;
under orders from Grand Duchess Tatiana, daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:45.000
of the Tsar, who was one of the many women&nbsp;
who claimed to have been raped by the mystic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:52.320
As of today, you can find pickled penises&nbsp;
purported to belong to Rasputin in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.320 --> 00:24:00.060
museums all over Russia. Of course, there&nbsp;
is a slight logical phallus...y with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:04.380
statement. Despite his reputation&nbsp;
as a lover of epic proportions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:11.040
so far as I know, God only gave Rasputin&nbsp;
*one* penis with which to do his holy work.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:14.760
Over the years, several of the&nbsp;
appendages have been tested to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:18.900
determine their providence. At least&nbsp;
one turned out to be a sea cucumber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:24.600
and another - a 12 inch behemoth of frankly&nbsp;
frightening proportions - was found to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:28.560
have belonged not to Rasputin,&nbsp;
but to a poorly endowed horse.

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:33.360
The truth is, while it's still&nbsp;
widely reported that Rasputin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:36.420
penis was severed after his death,&nbsp;
like so many things about his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:41.100
this one is probably just a myth - we know&nbsp;
for certain that the offending appendage was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:45.420
still attached during the autopsy, and&nbsp;
there’s evidence it was later removed.

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:51.540
By most accounts, Grigori Rasputin was a&nbsp;
pretty terrible human being, but his story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:56.580
really is fascinating. And if nothing else,&nbsp;
it remains proof of something many of us have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.580 --> 00:25:02.160
suspected for a long time - that you don't need&nbsp;
to have good personal hygiene to go far in life.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:05.460
Thanks for watching.

